1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,340
 Let's discuss Android overlays, overlays or RRO are one of the most essential techniques

2
00:00:07,340 --> 00:00:13,320
 for customizing Android.

3
00:00:13,320 --> 00:00:25,340
 So what exactly is an overlay or RRO?

4
00:00:25,340 --> 00:00:32,660
 In simple words, a runtime resource overlay or RRO is a package that changes the resource

5
00:00:32,660 --> 00:00:35,840
 values of a target package.

6
00:00:35,840 --> 00:00:45,040
 So for now, just imagine it as an APK with only resource files in it.

7
00:00:45,040 --> 00:00:49,920
 So what can be overlaid by using the RRO technique?

8
00:00:49,920 --> 00:00:56,980
 So RRO can technically overlay all the resource files like layouts, strings, colors, and

9
00:00:56,980 --> 00:00:59,520
 other XML resource files.

10
00:00:59,520 --> 00:01:03,440
 It can also overlay drawables and fonts.

11
00:01:03,440 --> 00:01:09,160
 So by doing this, you could dynamically change the look and feel of an application or

12
00:01:09,160 --> 00:01:11,360
 a system UI component.

13
00:01:11,360 --> 00:01:17,080
 So in this simple example, you could see that a blue Android is changed into a green

14
00:01:17,080 --> 00:01:19,760
 Android by using the resource overlays.

15
00:01:19,760 --> 00:01:23,400
 It could be either runtime or static overlays.

16
00:01:23,400 --> 00:01:26,840
 What can it be overlaid by RRO?

17
00:01:26,840 --> 00:01:33,880
 So you can overlay any source code that means the Java or Kotlin source code files and

18
00:01:33,880 --> 00:01:35,320
 it would be overlaid.

19
00:01:35,320 --> 00:01:40,000
 So for example, if you consider an application structure, you can overlay whatever coming

20
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:42,880
 under the resource folder.

21
00:01:42,880 --> 00:01:48,640
 And whatever coming under them, SRC folder, which is the Java and Kotlin classes, that

22
00:01:48,640 --> 00:01:54,120
 cannot be overlaid.

23
00:01:54,120 --> 00:01:58,840
 So let's have a closer look at the RRO overlay process.

24
00:01:58,840 --> 00:02:05,280
 So this diagram shows a high level overview of how an application is compiled, executed

25
00:02:05,280 --> 00:02:08,120
 and overlaid by an RRO.

26
00:02:08,120 --> 00:02:15,840
 So the top section shows how a normal Android application is converted into an Android APK

27
00:02:15,840 --> 00:02:17,200
 and it's executed.

28
00:02:17,200 --> 00:02:23,560
 And the bottom part shows how an RRO project gets converted into an RRO APK and gets

29
00:02:23,560 --> 00:02:26,880
 overlaid into the app process.

30
00:02:26,880 --> 00:02:34,360
 So in case of a normal Android application, we have mainly these files, right?

31
00:02:34,360 --> 00:02:41,240
 So the AndroidManifer.xml is one of the core files where we configure a lot of things for

32
00:02:41,240 --> 00:02:42,400
 the application.

33
00:02:42,400 --> 00:02:48,320
 And then we have the Java and Kotlin files where we tried the business logic and other

34
00:02:48,320 --> 00:02:49,480
 logics, right?

35
00:02:49,480 --> 00:02:55,440
 And also we have the resources folder where we have all the UI elements like the layouts

36
00:02:55,440 --> 00:03:00,680
 and other images or drawables which we use for the project.

37
00:03:00,680 --> 00:03:05,080
 The resource folder is the one which we are actually overlaid, right?

38
00:03:05,080 --> 00:03:10,280
 So when this project is built, it gets converted into an APK.

39
00:03:10,280 --> 00:03:15,560
 And if you open up an APK, you can see that within that there is the AndroidManifer

40
00:03:15,560 --> 00:03:20,720
 dot XML and then we have got the classes dot text file, which is basically the compiled

41
00:03:20,720 --> 00:03:23,640
 form of Java and Kotlin files.

42
00:03:23,640 --> 00:03:28,320
 And then we have got the resources dot ARSC file, which is basically a compiled form

43
00:03:28,320 --> 00:03:30,120
 of the resource files.

44
00:03:30,120 --> 00:03:34,920
 So during the execution, the app process will make use of the classes dot decks and app

45
00:03:34,920 --> 00:03:39,080
 resources dot ARSC to run the application.

46
00:03:39,080 --> 00:03:42,240
 So that's how a normal Android application runs.

47
00:03:42,240 --> 00:03:50,400
 So when there is a RRO in scope, then we have RRO resources dot ARSC and RRO ID map as

48
00:03:50,400 --> 00:03:52,600
 well within the process, okay?

49
00:03:52,600 --> 00:03:58,640
 So RRO resources dot ARSC is nothing but the overlayable resources, which is available

50
00:03:58,640 --> 00:04:05,880
 within the RRO APK and the RRO ID map is nothing but a table, which has a one or one mapping

51
00:04:05,880 --> 00:04:08,440
 the over label resources, okay?

52
00:04:08,440 --> 00:04:11,320
 So now let's have a look at the RRO section.

53
00:04:11,320 --> 00:04:19,040
 So a normal RRO project will consist of only an AndroidManifer.xml and the resource files.

54
00:04:19,040 --> 00:04:25,280
 So on compilation or on build process, this will get converted into an RRO APK.

55
00:04:25,280 --> 00:04:32,240
 And in the RRO APK, there will be the AndroidManifer.xml and the resources dot ARSC file.

56
00:04:32,240 --> 00:04:37,200
 So the resources dot ARSC file is nothing but the compiled form of resources file.

57
00:04:37,200 --> 00:04:42,640
 During the execution process, the app process will pick up this particular resources dot

58
00:04:42,640 --> 00:04:50,320
 ARSC and we'll look at the RRO ID map to get a corresponding resource ID for the over

59
00:04:50,320 --> 00:04:56,320
 label resource. And during the runtime, it will basically overlay the app resources dot

60
00:04:56,320 --> 00:05:04,080
 ARSC with the RRO resource dot ARSC. So please note that only the particular resource

61
00:05:04,080 --> 00:05:09,120
 will get overlayed. So for example, if you are only overlaying a layout XML, the only that

62
00:05:09,120 --> 00:05:14,600
 particular XML will get overlayed. The rest of the resources will be used from app resources

63
00:05:14,600 --> 00:05:20,240
 dot ARSC. So in short, during the runtime, the app process will look at the RRO ID

64
00:05:20,240 --> 00:05:28,080
 map and gets the right over label resource ID and overlay that resource on top of the app

65
00:05:28,080 --> 00:05:34,320
 resource dot ARSC. So that's how an RRO overlay process worked on a very high level.

66
00:05:34,320 --> 00:05:42,800
 Let's consider a real world application of static and dynamic RROs. Consider an example of

67
00:05:42,800 --> 00:05:49,840
 mobile manufacturers like Samsung, Motorola or Nokia. So they could use a static overlay for

68
00:05:49,840 --> 00:05:56,960
 creating different brands of mobile phones. So in this case, you can see brand X and brand Y

69
00:05:56,960 --> 00:06:05,440
 are created from a single ASP system image. So similarly, they could use dynamic RROs to create

70
00:06:05,440 --> 00:06:13,760
 different skins for a system. Okay. So as you could see here, on top of brand X and Y, they could

71
00:06:13,760 --> 00:06:24,000
 use dynamic RROs and create a blue skin and a red skin. So here is the difference between static

72
00:06:24,000 --> 00:06:31,840
 and dynamic RROs. The static RROs are applied at the build time and cannot be deactivated or

73
00:06:31,840 --> 00:06:40,400
 disabled later. Whereas dynamic RROs are enabled at runtime and it can be programmatically

74
00:06:40,400 --> 00:06:51,920
 enabled or disabled at a later stage.

